se,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"ST084\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States both express Reg. 26(2) exceptions: cash versus in-kind subscriptions and redemptions may have different cut-offs, and hedged versus unhedged classes may differ 'where the currency hedging operates at class level'. The class-level qualifier is present. The S.I. citation is an optional omission.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"Contrasts the regimes clearly. The 2026 provisions are 'express regulatory exceptions, not permissions requiring an individual waiver', while Q&A 1030 'described a case-by-case waiver application'. It does not carry the waiver requirement into 2026. For the limiting statement, it frames the provisions as exceptions to a 'common-dealing-deadline requirement' and says the route was 'not a general exemption for arbitrary class-specific deadlines'. That sentence is attached to Q&A 1030, which makes the limit somewhat indirect, but it suffices. Optional details (same frequencies and procedures, 42nd edition date) are omitted.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"Both required points are covered. The answer states both express 2026 exceptions with the class-level hedging qualifier. It distinguishes the old Q&A 1030 waiver route from the waiver-free 2026 exceptions. It confines the exceptions to the common-deadline framework. It does not say waivers are still required and does not permit arbitrary dealing frequencies, so there is no central error. No material local falsehood was found.\"}","ttft_ms":7242,"type":"result","duration_ms":11385,"uuid":"63558fe3-5fef-4c13-b8c5-33b98559a4d3","ttft_stream_ms":1210,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":1210,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
